WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.320
Swiss Avalanche Shelters built after 1951 put new housing on slopes once judged two hazardous.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.440
This is unintended consequences, episode 177.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:15.740
The winter of 1950 to 1951 killed 98 people and levelled entire clusters of buildings

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.940
in Iarolo, Andermat, and Vales.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.180
Many of those structures were relatively new homes and shops, put up in the decades before

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.420
despite local warnings.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:31.920
After the avalanches destroyed roughly 1,500 buildings across Switzerland, federal authorities

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.660
demanded national avalanche registers and hazard maps by June 1952.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.900
The explicit goal was to cut future deaths and property loss by making avalanche terrain

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.180
usable year round.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:50.100
The WSL Institute for Snow and Avalanche Research, SLF, answered by testing wood, aluminium,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.720
aluminum, wire rope, steel, and concrete support systems on the Dorfberg slope above Davos

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.980
from the mid-1950s onward.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:05.640
Professor Dr. R. Hayfully of ETH Zurich contributed designs for the first steel wire rope nets, field

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.060
tested on Schaffberg near Pontrasina in 1951.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.740
Central government funding rose sharply and practiced shifted from low stone walls and terraces

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.460
to engineered retaining structures.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:22.780
passengers. Provisional guidelines from 1955 became the 1961 official standard, Laouin

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.240
Verbao in Anbruch Gebiet. Engineers at the time saw physical barriers and the emerging

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.740
hazard maps as a rational way to turn restricted slopes into land that could hold permanent settlement

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:40.234
without repeating the losses of 1951. In the decades that followed, engineers installed more

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:45.934
than 500 kilometers of steel snow bridges, concrete galleries, and deflection dams to hold

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:51.654
snow on starting zones, and divert flows from roads and villages. Snowsheds, such as the

00:01:51.654 --> 00:01:57.254
seahorn gallery, shielded railway lines. Retaining structures alone eventually reached approximately

00:01:57.254 --> 00:02:04.294
600 kilometers. Color-coded hazard maps followed. The first, for Gadman, appeared in 1954.

00:02:05.094 --> 00:02:10.634
The Vengen map of 1960 introduced red and blue zones that prohibited or conditioned new construction.

00:02:11.454 --> 00:02:16.414
Municipalities and Cantons used those maps and the new physical barriers together, treating

00:02:16.414 --> 00:02:20.234
the combination as enough to permit settlement where none had been allowed before.

00:02:20.914 --> 00:02:25.634
Early reports from the SLF noted that the barriers performed as modeled during moderate

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:29.114
winters, and that record reinforced confidence in the approach.

00:02:29.794 --> 00:02:35.354
Cantinal building offices began approving projects on slopes whose red zone status had been downgraded

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:37.354
once the retaining structures were certified.

00:02:37.354 --> 00:02:42.414
The approval process rested on the assumption that the defenses would keep performing inside

00:02:42.414 --> 00:02:46.034
the design envelope, even as the number of protected sites grew.

00:02:47.021 --> 00:02:51.981
Once the barriers and maps were in place, new housing, hotels, and ski lifts appeared on

00:02:51.981 --> 00:02:54.221
slopes that local knowledge had kept empty.

00:02:54.881 --> 00:02:59.721
The absolute number of people and buildings inside potential avalanche paths rose even as

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:01.781
the calculated risk per structure fell.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:07.261
L. Concrete galleries and deflection dams more than 20 meters high protected traffic roots,

00:03:07.621 --> 00:03:12.021
and those same works created the perception that entire valleys had been rendered safe

00:03:12.021 --> 00:03:13.521
for permanent occupation.

00:03:14.181 --> 00:03:19.281
When extreme winters returned, the larger stock of exposed assets meant total damage figures

00:03:19.281 --> 00:03:23.641
could exceed those of 1951, despite fewer individual failures.

00:03:24.401 --> 00:03:29.661
The SLF later observed that many buildings put up after 1960 stood in zones that would

00:03:29.661 --> 00:03:32.841
of state off-limits without the combination of maps and structures.

00:03:33.641 --> 00:03:38.361
Second-order effects included higher maintenance costs for the defenses themselves and pressure

00:03:38.361 --> 00:03:41.881
to extend protection farther upslope as development crept higher.

00:03:42.681 --> 00:03:47.141
Third-order effects appeared when ski area operators and second home buyers treated the

00:03:47.141 --> 00:03:52.061
engineered slopes as permanently stable, and construction accelerated during the tourism

00:03:52.061 --> 00:03:54.721
boom of the 1960s and 1970s.

00:03:55.241 --> 00:03:56.761
The arithmetic ran this way.

00:03:56.761 --> 00:04:01.421
Each new retaining structure lowered the modeled probability for the parcels immediately

00:04:01.421 --> 00:04:05.841
downslope, and that lower figure justified the next round of building permits.

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:10.961
The permits increased the total number of structures whose residual risk, however small,

00:04:11.441 --> 00:04:13.601
was now multiplied across a larger base.

00:04:14.241 --> 00:04:19.201
Because the original hazard maps had been drawn before the barriers existed, later updates

00:04:19.201 --> 00:04:24.161
sometimes reclassified land only after development pressure had already shifted expectations

00:04:24.161 --> 00:04:26.501
about what counted as acceptable exposure.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:32.180
Hazard mapping continued and was refined, yet the existing stock of buildings remained.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.260
Some canton's tightened red zone rules, others permitted object protection,

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.220
such as concrete splitting wedges for individual structures.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.520
The cost of securing one hectare with retaining structures reached roughly one million Swiss

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.620
francs, and that figure prompted debate over whether further expansion should be subsidized.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:55.720
First, current practice at the SLF combines the original structural inventory with stricter

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.600
spatial planning that limits new exposure rather than attempting to armor ever larger

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.080
areas.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.440
Planners now track the cumulative footprint of protected zones so that each added defense

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.540
does not simply enlarge the area treated as developable.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.560
Maintenance budgets have also grown because the steel and concrete elements need periodic

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.140
inspection and replacement after heavy winters.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:22.000
measures. That ongoing fiscal commitment was not fully anticipated when the first kilometers

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.338
were installed. When physical barriers were paired with hazard maps after 1952, the maps

00:05:28.338 --> 00:05:33.758
were read as permission rather than prohibition once the barriers existed. Nobody had to intend

00:05:33.758 --> 00:05:38.558
that reading for the boundary of acceptable development to move. Decision makers who fund

00:05:38.558 --> 00:05:43.538
protective infrastructure should require an explicit cap on new exposure inside the protected

00:05:43.538 --> 00:05:48.938
footprint before any construction begins. Planners who update hazard zones after defenses

00:05:48.938 --> 00:05:53.858
are built should also publish the cumulative count of people and buildings now located inside

00:05:53.858 --> 00:05:59.598
those zones. So growth and absolute risk stays visible even when the risk on each parcel looks

00:05:59.598 --> 00:06:05.538
smaller. The interaction itself is the thing to watch. A physical measure and a regulatory map can

00:06:05.538 --> 00:06:10.518
shift what counts as buildable land without a vote to expand the settlement. How many current

00:06:10.518 --> 00:06:15.318
different infrastructure projects treat engineered safety as an invitation to intensify use rather

00:06:15.318 --> 00:06:19.998
than a boundary that must be actively defended. We'll leave it there for today. Thanks

00:06:19.998 --> 00:06:24.038
for thinking through this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good cautionary

